\title{#{\equiv_m} is a congruence}
\date{2026-04-10T13:51:04Z}
\tag{discrete}

\import{discrete-macros}

\taxon{Theorem}

\p{For any #{a,b,c,d \in \Z} and #{m \in \Z^+}, if #{a \equiv_m b} and #{c
\equiv_m d}, then

\ol{
  \li{#{a + c \equiv_m b + d}, and}
  \li{#{a - c \equiv_m b - d}, and}
  \li{#{ac \equiv_m bd}.}
}
(that is, #{\equiv_m} is a \em{congruence} with respect to addition, subtraction, and multiplication).}

\proof{
Let #{a,b,c,d} be arbitrary integers, and suppose #{a \equiv_m b} and #{c \equiv_m d}.
\ol{
  \li{By [a previous theorem](00DW) we can write #{a = b + jm} and #{c =
    d + km}. Then ##{a + c = (b + jm) + (c + km) = (b + c) +
      (j+k)m,} and thus by the same theorem in reverse, #{a + c
      \equiv_m b + d}.}
  \li{The proof for subtraction is very similar: ##{a - c = (b + jm) - (c + km) = (b - c) + (j-k)m,} so #{a - c \equiv_m b - d}.}
  \li{For multiplication, we can reason as follows: ##{ac = (b + jm)(c + km) = bc + m(\text{stuff}),} that is, when we expand the product, we get one #{bc} term and several other terms that all have an #{m} we can factor out. We don’t actually need to do all the algebra—we can see that #{ac} will be #{bd} plus #{m} times some integer, so #{ac \equiv_m bd}.}
}
}

